var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Steps","page":"Steps","text":"A Step is one unit of work: a shell command or a Julia function, with optional input and output paths. Build steps with @step:\n\n@step name = work\n@step name(inputs => outputs) = work\n@step name(inputs => outputs; threads=4, mem_mb=8000) = work\n@step name(inputs) = work            # inputs only, e.g. a validation step\n@step work                           # anonymous\n\ninputs and outputs are a path or a vector of paths. If any path contains a {wildcard}, @step returns a Rule instead (see rules).","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Shell-commands","page":"Steps","text":"A String or sh\"...\" is a shell command:\n\n@step unique_lines = sh\"sort data.txt | uniq -c > counts.txt\"\n@step unique_lines = \"sort data.txt | uniq -c > counts.txt\"     # the same\n\nCommands run with bash -e -o pipefail -c (sh -e -c if bash is missing). Strict mode means a failing command anywhere in the script, including the middle of a pipe, fails the step: bwa mem ... | samtools sort ... fails if bwa fails, instead of silently producing a truncated BAM.\n\nnote: Pipes into `head`\nUnder pipefail, zcat big.gz | head -n 4000 fails because zcat is killed by SIGPIPE when head stops reading. Write set +o pipefail; zcat big.gz | head -n 4000 > sub.fq for such commands.\n\nsh\"...\" does not interpolate $, so shell variables pass through. Use sh(\"...$(x)...\") or a string template when the command needs Julia values. Backtick Cmds work as well and run without a shell.","title":"Shell commands"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Templates-and-placeholders","page":"Steps","text":"When a step declares paths, its command is a template:\n\nplaceholder value\n{input}, {output} all inputs / outputs, space-separated\n{input[i]}, {output[i]} the i-th declared input / output\n{threads}, {mem_mb} the step's declared resources\n{sample} (any name) a wildcard value (rules only)\n\n@step bam([\"R1.fq\", \"R2.fq\"] => \"aln.bam\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input} | samtools sort -@ {threads} -o {output}\"\n\nPaths and values containing spaces or other special characters are shell-quoted automatically. An unknown placeholder is an error when the step is defined, so a typo cannot reach the shell. For a literal brace word, double the braces: awk '{{print}}' becomes awk '{print}'. ${VAR} is left to the shell. Braces around anything that is not a plain word, such as awk's {print $1}, need no escaping.","title":"Templates and placeholders"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Julia-functions","page":"Steps","text":"@step summarize = () -> sum(parse.(Int, readlines(\"numbers.txt\")))\n\nHow a function is called:\n\nWith declared paths, it receives them: f(inputs..., outputs...).\n@step filter_tsv(\"in.tsv\" => \"out.tsv\") = function (input, output)\n    lines = filter(l -> !startswith(l, \"#\"), readlines(input))\n    write(output, join(lines, \"\\n\"))\nend\nWithout paths, it receives the upstream value if it takes one argument, and is called with no arguments otherwise (see data flow).\n\nA function that returns a command has the command run as part of the step, which is handy when the command depends on something computed at run time:\n\n@step align_best = () -> sh(\"bwa mem $(pick_reference()) reads.fq > out.sam\")\n\nA function that returns a node, for example a ForEach, runs that node next. This is how to build part of a pipeline from data only known at run time.","title":"Julia functions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Deferred-calls","page":"Steps","text":"On the right-hand side of @step, a function call runs when the step runs, not when the pipeline is built:\n\n@step load = read_big_table(\"x.csv\")     # read_big_table runs inside the pipeline\n\nCommand builders are the exception and run immediately, so the command is known for display and change tracking: sh(...), string(...), *, Cmd(...) and pipeline(...).","title":"Deferred calls"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#What-declaring-paths-buys-you","page":"Steps","text":"For a step with declared outputs:\n\nit is skipped when every output exists, no input is newer than the oldest output, and the outputs were made by the same command and inputs;\nthe output directories are created before it runs;\nit fails clearly when an input is missing, or when it finishes without creating an output;\noutput files it wrote are removed if it fails, so a partial file never looks finished;\ntwo different steps that declare the same outputs run only once in a run.\n\nSteps without outputs always run: there is nothing on disk to be up to date.","title":"What declaring paths buys you"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Resources","page":"Steps","text":"threads, mem_mb (MiB) and timeout (seconds) tell the scheduler what a step needs, and they are limits while it runs:\n\n@step sort_bam(\"aln.bam\" => \"sorted.bam\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000, timeout=3600) =\n    \"samtools sort -@ {threads} -m 1500M -o {output} {input}\"\n\nA step occupies threads of the jobs CPU slots given to run and starts when its mem_mb fits in the free memory. A command that uses more memory than its mem_mb, or runs longer than its timeout, is terminated (a Julia function cannot be; see julia below). {threads} and {mem_mb} are filled in when the step starts. with_resources sets defaults for every step inside a node that does not declare its own. See Resource limits and safety.","title":"Resources"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/steps/#Julia-code-in-a-separate-process","page":"Steps","text":"Julia functions run inside your Julia session, where they cannot be limited or stopped. For heavy Julia work, run a script with julia: it gets its own process, limited like any command.\n\n@step counts(\"reads.tsv\" => \"counts.tsv\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000) =\n    julia(\"scripts/count.jl {input} {output}\")\n\nNext: Composing pipelines.","title":"Julia code in a separate process"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foreach/#Fan-out-with-ForEach","page":"Fan-out with ForEach","text":"ForEach builds one branch per item when the pipeline runs. Use it when the work is driven by a list or a set of files and you want ordinary Julia code in each branch. Use rules when output file names should drive the work and dependencies between files matter.","title":"Fan-out with ForEach"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foreach/#Over-a-collection","page":"Fan-out with ForEach","text":"samples = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"]\n\nper_sample = ForEach(samples) do s\n    @step count = sh(\"zcat raw/$(s).fq.gz | wc -l\")\nend\n\nresults = run(per_sample)\nresults.value              # one stdout per sample, in order\n\nThe block returns a step or node; a shell command or function is lifted to a step. Log lines are tagged with the item: count[A]. Any iterable works: ForEach(1:10), ForEach(eachrow(df)), ForEach(readlines(\"samples.txt\")).","title":"Over a collection"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foreach/#Over-files","page":"Fan-out with ForEach","text":"ForEach(\"fastq/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\") do sample\n    @step trim = sh(\"fastp -i fastq/$(sample)_R1.fq.gz -o trim/$(sample).fq.gz\")\nend\n\nThe pattern is matched when the pipeline runs, so it sees files that earlier steps created. Several wildcards arrive as several arguments, in order:\n\nForEach(\"data/{project}/{sample}.csv\") do project, sample\n    @step load = () -> process(\"data/$project/$sample.csv\")\nend\n\nA pattern that matches nothing is reported as a failure (:no_matches), because it almost always means a wrong path. An empty collection is not a failure.","title":"Over files"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foreach/#Reducing-branch-values","page":"Fan-out with ForEach","text":"The value of a ForEach is the vector of branch values, so >> into a function reduces it:\n\ntotal = ForEach(samples) do s\n    @step reads = () -> countlines(\"raw/$(s).fq\") ÷ 4\nend >> sum\n\nFor large data, return paths rather than big objects, and let the reducer read the files one at a time.","title":"Reducing branch values"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/foreach/#Branches-and-resources","page":"Fan-out with ForEach","text":"All branches run in parallel within the jobs budget of run. Declare what heavy steps need, so that they queue instead of overloading the machine:\n\nForEach(samples) do s\n    @step align(; threads=8, mem_mb=16_000) =\n        sh(\"bwa mem -t 8 ref.fa raw/$(s).fq | samtools sort -o bam/$(s).bam\")\nend\n\nNext: Control flow.","title":"Branches and resources"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/composition/#Composing-pipelines","page":"Composing pipelines","text":"Four operators draw the graph. Any shell command (sh\"...\", a String or a Cmd) or function next to a node is lifted to a step automatically.\n\noperator meaning node\na >> b run b after a succeeds Sequence\na & b run a and b at the same time Parallel\na | b run b only if a fails Fallback\na^n retry a up to n times Retry\n\nfetch >> (qc & align) >> report\ndownload^3 | use_cached_copy\n\nwarning: Precedence\nJulia gives >> higher precedence than &, and & higher than |. So a & b >> c means a & (b >> c): write (a & b) >> c when c should wait for both.\n\nComposition is cheap and has no side effects: nothing runs until run is called. A node can be reused in several places; a node that appears in several branches of one pipeline runs once, and every branch sees its result.","title":"Composing pipelines"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/composition/#Data-flow","page":"Composing pipelines","text":"Every node has a value, and >> passes it to the next node:\n\nnode value\nstep with declared outputs the output paths (Vector{String})\nother shell step its stdout (String)\nother function step its return value\na & b, ForEach the vector of branch values, in order\na >> b the value of b\nWorkflow the paths of its targets\n\nA function step without declared paths receives the upstream value when it takes one argument, and is called with none otherwise:\n\nhalf = @step half = () -> 21\ndouble = @step double = x -> 2x\nannounce = @step announce = () -> println(\"done\")\n\nrun(half >> double >> announce)        # announce takes no argument: fine\nrun(half >> double).value              # 42\n\nParallel branches produce a vector, which makes reductions direct:\n\nsizes = ForEach([\"a.txt\", \"b.txt\"]) do f\n    @step size = () -> filesize(f)\nend\nrun(sizes >> sum).value                # total size\n\nFor file-based pipelines the paths are usually all you need: a step after another one simply reads the files it declares.","title":"Data flow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/composition/#Nodes-as-values","page":"Composing pipelines","text":"All composition results are ordinary Julia values, so pipelines can be built with ordinary code:\n\nper_chrom = [@step call([\"sample.bam\"] => \"calls/chr$c.vcf\") =\n                 \"bcftools mpileup -r chr$c -f ref.fa {input} | bcftools call -mv -o {output}\"\n             for c in 1:22]\n\npipeline = align >> Parallel(per_chrom) >> merge_vcfs\n\nSequence(nodes...) and Parallel(nodes...) (or a vector of nodes) are the function forms of >> and &.\n\nNext: Wildcard rules and workflows.","title":"Nodes as values"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Steps","page":"API","text":"","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Composition","page":"API","text":"The operators >>, &, | and ^ build these nodes; see Composing pipelines.","title":"Composition"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Rules-and-workflows","page":"API","text":"","title":"Rules and workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Running","page":"API","text":"","title":"Running"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Inspection-and-state","page":"API","text":"","title":"Inspection and state"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.SimplePipelines","page":"API","text":"SimplePipelines\n\nIncremental, parallel pipelines for shell commands and Julia functions, with Snakemake-style wildcard rules.\n\nusing SimplePipelines\n\n# explicit steps\nprep  = @step prep([] => \"data.txt\") = \"seq 1 100 > {output}\"\ntotal = @step total(\"data.txt\" => \"total.txt\") = \"paste -sd+ {input} | bc > {output}\"\nrun(prep >> total)\n\n# wildcard rules: one step per sample found on disk\ntrim  = @rule trim(\"raw/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{s}.fq.gz\") = \"fastp -i {input} -o {output}\"\nalign = @rule align(\"trim/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"bam/{s}.bam\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input} | samtools sort -o {output}\"\nrun(trim >> align)\n\nSee @step, @rule, Workflow, run and check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.SimplePipelines"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Step","page":"API","text":"Step(work)\nStep(name::Symbol, work; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0)\nStep(name::Symbol, work, inputs, outputs; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0)\n\nOne unit of work. Usually built with @step.\n\nwork is one of:\n\na shell command: sh\"...\" or a String (run with bash -e -o pipefail);\na Cmd (backticks);\njulia(\"script.jl ...\"): Julia code in a separate, limited process;\na Julia function (runs inside this Julia process).\n\nthreads, mem_mb (MiB) and timeout (seconds) are limits: a command that uses more memory or runs longer is terminated, and steps only start when their resources fit. A Julia function runs inside this process and cannot be limited or stopped; use julia for heavy Julia code.\n\nWith declared inputs/outputs (the path form), a shell template may use {input}, {output}, {input[i]}, {output[i]}, {threads} and {mem_mb}, and a function is called with the paths: f(inputs..., outputs...). Without paths, a function receives the upstream value when it takes one argument, and nothing otherwise.\n\nDeclared paths make the step incremental: it is skipped when its outputs exist, are newer than its inputs and were made by the same command. Output directories are created before the step runs, and outputs left behind by a failed run are removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Step"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.@step","page":"API","text":"@step name = work\n@step name(inputs => outputs; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0) = work\n@step name(inputs) = work\n@step work\n\nCreate a named Step. When a path contains a {wildcard}, the result is a Rule (a step template) instead.\n\nwork is a shell command (sh\"...\", a String template, a Cmd), Julia code in a separate process (julia(\"script.jl ...\")) or a function. A function call on the right-hand side is deferred until the step runs (@step load = read_table(\"x.csv\")), except for command builders (sh(...), string(...), *, Cmd(...), pipeline(...), julia(...)). A function that returns a command has that command run. threads, mem_mb (MiB) and timeout (seconds) are limits; see Step.\n\n@step hello = sh\"echo hello\"\n@step sorted(\"data.txt\" => \"sorted.txt\") = \"sort {input} > {output}\"\n@step bam([\"r1.fq\", \"r2.fq\"] => \"a.bam\"; threads=8) = \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input} > {output}\"\n@step stats(\"a.bam\" => \"a.txt\") = (bam, txt) -> write(txt, string(filesize(bam)))\n@step align(\"raw/{s}.fq\" => \"bam/{s}.bam\") = \"bwa mem ref.fa {input} > {output}\"   # a Rule\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.@step"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.@sh_str","page":"API","text":"sh\"command\"\nsh(command)\n\nA shell command. The literal form does not interpolate $, so shell variables such as $HOME reach the shell untouched. Use sh(\"... $(x) ...\") when the command needs Julia values.\n\nsh\"sort data.txt | uniq -c > counts.txt\"\nsh(\"bwa mem -t $(n) ref.fa $(sample).fq > $(sample).sam\")\n\nShell commands run with bash -e -o pipefail, so a failure anywhere in a pipe fails the step. See also Shell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.@sh_str"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.sh","page":"API","text":"sh(command::AbstractString) -> Shell\n\nSame as sh\"...\", but takes an ordinary string so the command can interpolate Julia values: sh(\"samtools index $(bam)\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.sh"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Shell","page":"API","text":"Shell(script)\n\nA shell script, run with bash -e -o pipefail -c script (or sh -e -c script when bash is not installed). Strict mode means a failing command, including one in the middle of a pipe, fails the step instead of being silently ignored.\n\nBuild one with @sh_str or sh.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Shell"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.julia","page":"API","text":"julia(arguments)\n\nWork that runs Julia code in a separate process, so it can be limited, measured and terminated like any command (Julia functions run inside this process and cannot be). arguments are the arguments of julia, with the same placeholders as shell templates; the process is started with this Julia, the active project, --threads={threads} and, when the step declares mem_mb, a matching --heap-size-hint. Your own flags come last and take precedence.\n\n@step counts(\"reads.tsv\" => \"counts.tsv\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000) =\n    julia(\"scripts/count.jl {input} {output}\")\n\nIn the script, the paths are in ARGS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.julia"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.with_resources","page":"API","text":"with_resources(node; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0)\n\nDefault resources for every step inside node that does not declare its own: threads, mem_mb (a limit in MiB) and timeout (seconds). See Resource limits for how they are enforced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.with_resources"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.AbstractNode","page":"API","text":"AbstractNode\n\nSupertype of everything run can execute: Step, the composite nodes built by >>, &, | and ^, ForEach and Workflow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.AbstractNode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Sequence","page":"API","text":"Sequence(nodes...)\na >> b\n\nRun nodes one after another, stopping at the first failure. Each node receives the value of the previous one. The value of a sequence is the value of its last node.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Sequence"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Parallel","page":"API","text":"Parallel(nodes...)\na & b\n\nRun nodes concurrently; every node receives the same input. The value is the vector of the node values, in order. A failing branch does not stop the others.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Parallel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Retry","page":"API","text":"Retry(node, max_attempts=3; delay=0)\nnode^n\n\nRun node again after a failure, up to max_attempts times, sleeping delay seconds between attempts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Retry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Fallback","page":"API","text":"Fallback(primary, fallback)\nprimary | fallback\n\nRun fallback only when primary fails.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Fallback"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Branch","page":"API","text":"Branch(condition, if_true, if_false)\n\nRun one of two nodes. condition is called when the branch is reached, with the upstream value if it accepts one argument, and must return a Bool. See also @branch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Branch"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.@branch","page":"API","text":"@branch condition if_true if_false\n\nBranch with a condition written inline; it is evaluated when the branch is reached. @branch isfile(\"cache.bin\") load compute is Branch(() -> isfile(\"cache.bin\"), load, compute).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.@branch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Timeout","page":"API","text":"Timeout(node, seconds)\n\nFail if node has not finished within seconds. Shell commands still running at the deadline are terminated, including their child processes; steps that have not started yet are not started.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Timeout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Force","page":"API","text":"Force(node)\n\nRun every step inside node even when its outputs are up to date.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Force"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.ForEach","page":"API","text":"ForEach(items) do item ... end\nForEach(\"pattern/{wildcard}.ext\") do wildcard... end\n\nFan out: build one branch per item, or per file matching the pattern, when the pipeline runs. The block returns a step or node (a shell command or function is lifted to a step). Branches run in parallel; the value is the vector of branch values.\n\nWith a pattern, the block receives the wildcard values in order of appearance, and step names in the log are tagged with them (align[sample=A]). A pattern that matches no files is a failure; an empty collection is not.\n\nForEach([\"A\", \"B\"]) do s\n    @step count = sh(\"wc -l $(s).txt\")\nend\n\nForEach(\"fastq/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\") do sample\n    @step trim = sh(\"fastp -i fastq/$(sample)_R1.fq.gz -o trim/$(sample).fq.gz\")\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.ForEach"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Pipeline","page":"API","text":"Pipeline(node; name=\"pipeline\")\n\nA node with a name, shown in the log of run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Pipeline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Rule","page":"API","text":"Rule(name, inputs, outputs, work; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0)\n\nA step template whose paths contain {wildcards}. Usually built with @rule or with @step when the paths contain wildcards.\n\ninputs: patterns, possibly gather(...) entries.\noutputs: patterns; every output must use the same wildcards, and every (non-gather) input wildcard must appear in the outputs.\nwork: a shell template (String or sh\"...\") with placeholders {input}, {output}, {input[i]}, {output[i]}, {threads}, {mem_mb} and {wildcard} ({{x}} is a literal {x}), julia(\"script.jl {input} {output}\") with the same placeholders, or a function called as f(inputs..., outputs...).\nthreads, mem_mb (MiB) and timeout (seconds): limits of every step the rule makes, as for Step.\n\nA rule produces nothing by itself: use it in a Workflow, run it, or compose rules with >>/& (which merges them into one Workflow).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Rule"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.@rule","page":"API","text":"@rule name(inputs => outputs; threads=0, mem_mb=0, timeout=0) = work\n\nCreate a Rule: a step template with {wildcard} patterns.\n\n@rule trim(\"raw/{s}_R1.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{s}.fq.gz\"; threads=4) =\n    \"fastp -w {threads} -i {input} -o {output}\"\n@rule merge(gather(\"bam/{s}.bam\") => \"all.vcf\") = \"bcftools mpileup -f ref.fa {input} | bcftools call -mv -o {output}\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.@rule"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.gather","page":"API","text":"gather(pattern)\n\nRule input that collects every value of the wildcards that do not appear in the outputs. The values are found by tracing the rules back to existing source files, so gather works for files that other rules have not produced yet.\n\n@rule merge(gather(\"bam/{sample}.bam\") => \"all.vcf\") =\n    \"bcftools mpileup -f ref.fa {input} | bcftools call -mv -o {output}\"\n\nA function rule receives a gathered input as a Vector{String}; in a shell template {input[i]} expands to the space-separated list.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.gather"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Workflow","page":"API","text":"Workflow(rules...; targets=String[], name=\"workflow\", filter=nothing)\n\nA set of Rules and concrete Steps with declared outputs, plus the targets to build. A Workflow is a node: it is resolved into concrete steps when it runs, so it sees files produced by earlier steps.\n\nTargets are concrete paths or patterns. A pattern target such as \"bam/{s}.bam\" builds every instance whose source files exist: wildcard values are discovered by tracing the rules back to files on disk. Without targets, the workflow builds the outputs of every rule that no other rule consumes (and runs rules that have no outputs once per matching input).\n\nfilter(w::Wildcards) -> Bool drops discovered wildcard values, e.g. filter = w -> w.sample != \"control\".\n\nThe value of a Workflow (passed downstream with >>) is the vector of target paths.\n\ntrim  = @rule trim(\"raw/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{s}.fq.gz\") = \"fastp -i {input} -o {output}\"\nalign = @rule align(\"trim/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"bam/{s}.bam\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input} | samtools sort -o {output}\"\n\nrun(Workflow(trim, align))                            # every sample with raw data\nrun(Workflow(trim, align; targets=\"bam/A.bam\"))       # just sample A\n\nSee also @workflow, plan, check.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Workflow"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.@workflow","page":"API","text":"@workflow \"name\" begin\n    rules, steps and targets...\nend\n\nBuild a Workflow from a block. Every expression that evaluates to a Rule, a Step or a vector of them is added; a string or vector of strings (that is not assigned to a variable) is a target. Assignments of other values are allowed for local helpers.\n\nwf = @workflow \"rnaseq\" begin\n    samples = [\"A\", \"B\"]\n    @rule trim(\"raw/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{s}.fq.gz\") = \"fastp -i {input} -o {output}\"\n    @rule align(\"trim/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"bam/{s}.bam\") = \"bwa mem ref.fa {input} > {output}\"\n    expand(\"bam/{s}.bam\"; s=samples)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.@workflow"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.expand","page":"API","text":"expand(template; wildcard=values, ...) -> Vector{String}\nexpand(templates::AbstractVector; wildcard=values, ...) -> Vector{String}\n\nFill a pattern with every combination of the given values. The last wildcard varies fastest; with several templates, all combinations of the first come before the second. A single string or number counts as one value.\n\nexpand(\"bam/{sample}.bam\"; sample=[\"A\", \"B\"])        # [\"bam/A.bam\", \"bam/B.bam\"]\nexpand(\"{s}_R{r}.fq\"; s=[\"A\", \"B\"], r=1:2)           # [\"A_R1.fq\", \"A_R2.fq\", \"B_R1.fq\", \"B_R2.fq\"]\nexpand([\"bam/{s}.bam\", \"qc/{s}.html\"]; s=\"A\")       # [\"bam/A.bam\", \"qc/A.html\"]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.expand"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.discover","page":"API","text":"discover(pattern) -> Vector{Wildcards}\n\nFind existing files matching pattern and return their wildcard values, sorted by path. Use it to check that a pattern matches what you expect, or to build sample lists:\n\ndiscover(\"raw/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\")          # [Wildcards(sample=\"A\"), ...]\nsamples = [w.sample for w in discover(\"raw/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\")]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.discover"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.Wildcards","page":"API","text":"Wildcards\n\nThe wildcard values of one match, as returned by discover and passed to a Workflow filter. Read values as properties or by name:\n\nw.sample       # \"A\"\nw[\"sample\"]    # \"A\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.Wildcards"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.plan","page":"API","text":"plan(rules...; targets=String[], filter=nothing)\nplan(workflow::Workflow)\n\nResolve rules into the concrete DAG that would run, without running anything. Print it with print_dag, run it with run, or compose it with other nodes. Errors with an explanation when a target cannot be built.\n\nprint_dag(plan(trim, align; targets=\"bam/A.bam\"))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Base.run","page":"API","text":"run(node; jobs, mem_mb, min_free_mb=0, nice=10, guard=true, force=false, verbose=true,\n    state_path=\".pipeline_state\") -> RunResult\nrun(rules...; targets, filter, kwargs...) -> RunResult\n\nRun a pipeline and return a RunResult: one StepResult per step, the overall success, and the pipeline's value.\n\njobs: CPU slots (default: the CPUs this process may use). A step occupies as many slots as its threads, so at most jobs single-threaded steps run at once, however the pipeline is nested.\nmem_mb: memory budget in MiB for the steps running at once (default: 80% of the memory this process may use; 0 = no budget). A step is charged its mem_mb, or 256 MiB when it declares none; a larger declaration is clamped to the budget.\nmin_free_mb: memory to keep free (default 0: automatic, 5% of memory between 256 and 2048 MiB). Below twice this, new steps wait; below it, the pipeline's own commands are stopped and queued again before the computer starts swapping.\nnice: scheduling priority of the commands (0–19, default 10), so the computer stays responsive.\nguard: enforce limits with the resource guard (default, except on Windows where it is not available). guard=false does not enforce memory limits, step timeouts or memory pressure, and cannot clean up if Julia is killed; Timeout nodes and the cleanup at the end of each step still work.\nforce: run every step even when it is up to date.\nverbose: print progress.\nstate_path: where recipes of built outputs are remembered.\n\nSteps with declared outputs are skipped when up to date; the others always run. A failing step stops its branch; independent branches keep going, so one bad sample does not stop the others. See Resource limits for what the guard enforces.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.run"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.check","page":"API","text":"check(node; kwargs...) -> RunResult\ncheck(rules...; targets, filter, kwargs...) -> RunResult\n\nWork out what run would do without running anything: expand ForEach nodes, resolve workflows, and report every step as :planned (with the reason it would run), :skipped (up to date) or :failed (a problem such as an input that does not exist and that no step produces). Takes the same keywords as run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.check"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.RunResult","page":"API","text":"RunResult\n\nWhat run and check return: a vector of StepResults in completion order, plus\n\nsuccess: whether the pipeline as a whole succeeded (a Fallback that recovered counts as success even though its primary failed); also success(result);\nvalue: the value of the pipeline, e.g. the return value of a final function step;\nduration: wall time in seconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.RunResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.StepResult","page":"API","text":"StepResult\n\nThe record of one step in a run.\n\nstep: the Step.\nlabel: display name, including wildcard values (align[sample=A]).\nstatus: :done, :skipped (up to date), :failed, or :planned (from check).\nsuccess: status != :failed.\nduration: seconds.\npeak_mb: peak memory of the step's command in MiB, nothing when not measured (function steps, skipped and planned steps, systems where memory is not measured).\ninputs, outputs: the declared paths.\nresult: stdout for a shell step, the return value for a function, a StepFailure when failed, the reason when planned, nothing when skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.StepResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.StepFailure","page":"API","text":"StepFailure(kind, message; detail=\"\")\n\nWhy a step failed. kind is one of:\n\nkind meaning\n:process_failed the command exited with a non-zero status, left background processes running, or was killed from outside\n:memory_limit the command used more memory than its mem_mb and was terminated\n:out_of_memory the computer ran out of memory for this step (or the system's OOM killer stopped it)\n:timed_out the step's timeout or an enclosing Timeout expired\n:stopped the run was interrupted, or the resource guard exited unexpectedly\n:exception Julia code threw (message and the top of the stack trace)\n:template a placeholder cannot be filled, e.g. {mem_mb} without a declared mem_mb\n:missing_input a declared input does not exist\n:missing_output the step finished without creating a declared output\n:stdout_limit the command printed more than 32 MiB; redirect large output to a file\n:no_matches a ForEach pattern matched no files\n:unresolved a Workflow could not work out how to build a target\n\ndetail holds extra context such as the end of stderr.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.StepFailure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.print_dag","page":"API","text":"print_dag([io,] node; expand=true)\n\nPrint the pipeline as a tree. With expand=true, ForEach nodes are expanded and Workflows resolved against the files on disk, so you see the concrete steps that would run, with their paths and commands. A sub-DAG shared by several branches is printed once.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.print_dag"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.steps","page":"API","text":"steps(node) -> Vector{Step}\n\nThe distinct steps of a node, dependencies first. ForEach and Workflow nodes are expanded only when they run, so they contribute no steps here; use plan to resolve a workflow first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.steps"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.count_steps","page":"API","text":"count_steps(node) -> Int\n\nNumber of distinct steps in a node; see steps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.count_steps"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.is_fresh","page":"API","text":"is_fresh(step::Step; state_path=\".pipeline_state\") -> Bool\n\nWhether step would be skipped as up to date. Only steps with declared outputs can be up to date; see Step for the rules.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.is_fresh"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#SimplePipelines.clear_state!","page":"API","text":"clear_state!(state_path=\".pipeline_state\")\n\nForget the recorded recipes. Outputs are then judged by timestamps only until steps run again; use run(...; force=true) to rebuild everything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SimplePipelines.clear_state!"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"This page builds a small pipeline: make a data file, sort it, then compute two summaries in parallel.\n\nprepare ── sort_file ──┬── count_lines\n                       └── count_bytes","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Steps-declare-what-they-read-and-write","page":"Getting started","text":"using SimplePipelines\n\nprepare = @step prepare([] => \"data.txt\") = \"seq 3 -1 1 > {output}\"\n\nsort_file = @step sort_file(\"data.txt\" => \"sorted.txt\") =\n    \"sort {input} > {output}\"\n\ncount_lines = @step count_lines(\"sorted.txt\" => \"lines.txt\") =\n    \"wc -l < {input} > {output}\"\n\ncount_bytes = @step count_bytes(\"sorted.txt\" => \"bytes.txt\") =\n    \"wc -c < {input} > {output}\"\n\nname(inputs => outputs) = command declares the files. In the command, {input} and {output} are replaced by those paths. Declaring files is what makes the pipeline incremental and safe: the step is skipped when its outputs are up to date, its output directory is created for it, and it fails clearly if an input is missing or an output was not produced.","title":"Steps declare what they read and write"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Compose-with-and-and","page":"Getting started","text":"pipeline = prepare >> sort_file >> (count_lines & count_bytes)\n\n>> means \"then\" and & means \"at the same time\". Keep the parentheses: in Julia, >> binds tighter than &.","title":"Compose with >> and &"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Look-before-you-run","page":"Getting started","text":"print_dag(pipeline)      # the structure, with paths and commands\ncheck(pipeline)          # what would run, and why\n\ncheck runs nothing. It reports each step as planned (with the reason, such as missing output sorted.txt), up to date, or as a problem, such as an input that no step produces.","title":"Look before you run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Run","page":"Getting started","text":"results = run(pipeline)\n\n═══ Pipeline: pipeline ═══\n▶ prepare\n    $ seq 3 -1 1 > data.txt\n✓ prepare (0.01s)\n▶ sort_file\n    $ sort data.txt > sorted.txt\n✓ sort_file (0.01s)\n...\n═══ 4 done, 0 up to date, 0 failed in 0.05s ═══\n\nrun returns a RunResult: a vector with one StepResult per step, plus the overall success and the pipeline's value.\n\nsuccess(results)                   # true\nresults[2].label, results[2].status\n\nRun it again and every step is skipped: the outputs exist and nothing changed. Touch data.txt, or edit a command, and exactly the affected steps run again.","title":"Run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Julia-functions-are-steps-too","page":"Getting started","text":"A function can be a step. With declared paths it receives them as arguments; the last step below receives the value of the previous one.\n\ntotal = @step total(\"lines.txt\" => \"report.txt\") = (lines, report) ->\n    write(report, \"lines: \" * strip(read(lines, String)))\n\nshow_report = @step show_report = paths -> read(only(paths), String)\n\nrun(pipeline >> total >> show_report).value     # \"lines: 3\"","title":"Julia functions are steps too"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/getting-started/#Many-samples:-rules","page":"Getting started","text":"Real pipelines repeat the same steps for many files. Instead of writing one step per file, write a rule with a {wildcard}:\n\nalign = @rule align(\"reads/{sample}.fq\" => \"bam/{sample}.bam\") =\n    \"bwa mem ref.fa {input} | samtools sort -o {output}\"\n\nrun(align)        # once for every reads/*.fq on disk\n\nThat is the subject of Wildcard rules and workflows.\n\nNext: Steps.","title":"Many samples: rules"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/migration/#Migrating-from-0.3","page":"Migrating from 0.3","text":"Version 0.4 is a redesign around one data model (values flow with >>, files are declared on steps) and one resolution model for wildcards. Most pipelines need small edits.","title":"Migrating from 0.3"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/migration/#Removed,-and-what-to-use-instead","page":"Migrating from 0.3","text":"0.3 0.4\na |> f, Reduce(f, a) a >> f: a parallel node's value is the vector of branch values\na .>> b put b inside the ForEach block: ForEach(xs) do x; step(x) >> b end\na >>> b ForEach branches no longer receive the item as input; use the block argument\nRuleChain (r1 |> r2), RuleValuePipe r1 >> r2 (a Workflow), then >> f\nresolve(rules, targets) plan(rules...; targets) or run(rules...; targets)\ncheck(rule, target), explain(wf; target) plan(rule; targets=target) and print_dag, or check(x) for a dry run\nrun(x; dry_run=true) check(x)\n@targets \"p/{s}\" s=[...] expand(\"p/{s}\"; s=[...]), or a pattern target\nwildcards_filter= filter=\nsh(() -> cmd) () -> sh(cmd): a function returning a command runs it\nsh_pipe(a, b) pipeline(a, b), or one sh\"a | b\"\nshell_raw\"...\" raw\"...\"; sh\"...\" already leaves $ alone\nfe(...) ForEach(...)\nFilePath, materialize, materialize_table, auto-spill values are passed as they are; write large data to files and pass paths\nmemory_budget_mb, thread_budget mem_mb, and jobs counts threads\nreport= keyword inspect the returned RunResult\nNoWork, Pipe, SameInputPipe, BroadcastPipe, Resources, Resourced internal or gone\nStep{F}, StepResult{...} type parameters plain Step, StepResult","title":"Removed, and what to use instead"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/migration/#Behaviour-changes","page":"Migrating from 0.3","text":"Steps without declared outputs always run. In 0.3 they were skipped on later runs and passed the string \"up to date (not re-run)\" downstream.\nA changed command reruns the step, even when the outputs are newer than the inputs. The state file is now a small text file; the old 8 MB binary file is ignored and replaced.\nShell commands use bash -e -o pipefail. A failure inside a pipe now fails the step. Commands like zcat x.gz | head need set +o pipefail; in front.\nOutputs of a failed step are deleted, and output directories are created automatically, so mkdir -p in commands is no longer needed.\nWorkflows rebuild stale targets. In 0.3 an existing target was treated as done even when its inputs had changed.\nFunction steps adapt to their arity. A zero-argument function after another step is called without arguments instead of failing.\nA function rule receives one argument per declared input, then the outputs (f(input, output) as before, f(r1, r2, output) for two inputs). The (inputs, outputs, wildcards) builder form is gone; use a string template with {wildcard} placeholders, or derive values from the paths.\njobs is a global cap on busy CPU threads (default: all CPU threads), counting each step's threads. In 0.3 it limited branches per parallel node, so nested parallelism multiplied.\nrun returns a RunResult. It still behaves as a vector of StepResults, and adds success, value and duration. StepResult gained label and status.\nErrors in user code are reported as failed steps instead of being thrown, for example a ForEach block that returns nothing, or a Branch condition that throws.\nThe operators do not change Base behaviour any more. a  b is Base's AndCmds again. Use sh\"a\" & sh\"b\", or wrap commands in steps.\nResources are limits. mem_mb (MiB) and the new timeout are enforced by a resource guard process: a command that uses more memory or runs longer is terminated. Steps also wait for free memory, and the pipeline's commands are stopped and requeued before the computer runs out of memory. run(...; guard=false) turns this off. See Resource limits and safety.\nmem_mb defaults to 80% of the memory the process may use (it was half of the RAM), and steps without mem_mb are charged 256 MiB. jobs defaults to the CPUs the process may use, which respects container and cluster limits.\n{threads} and {mem_mb} are filled in when a step starts, from what it was granted; a mem_mb above the run's budget is lowered to it. {mem_mb} in a step that declares no mem_mb is an error.\nCommands must not leave processes behind. A step whose command exits while its background jobs still run fails, and the jobs are terminated; end such scripts with wait.\nCommands run at nice 10 and see OMP_NUM_THREADS and similar variables set to their threads.\nJulia 1.10 is the minimum version.","title":"Behaviour changes"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Tests","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=. -t 4 -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nThe suite lives in test/, one file per area (execution.jl, rules.jl, scheduler.jl, …), all included from test/runtests.jl. Every test runs in its own temporary directory, so state files and outputs never collide. Run it with one and with several threads: the scheduler and memoization tests exercise concurrency. The first testset checks the package for method ambiguities and unbound type parameters.\n\nCoverage:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test(coverage=true)'","title":"Tests"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Documentation","page":"Development","text":"julia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate(); include(\"docs/make.jl\")'\n\ndocs/make.jl runs the doctests in docs/src/doctests.md during the build. Set DOCUMENTER_DOCTEST=only to run only the doctests, or false to skip them. The Documentation workflow builds the docs, with doctests, on every pull request.","title":"Documentation"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Adding-a-node-type","page":"Development","text":"A node is a subtype of AbstractNode with an exec method. Implement the pieces the runtime and display use:\n\nstruct Repeat <: SimplePipelines.AbstractNode\n    node::SimplePipelines.AbstractNode\n    times::Int\nend\n\nfunction SimplePipelines.exec(r::Repeat, rt, scope, input)\n    out = SimplePipelines.run_node(r.node, rt, scope, input)\n    for _ in 2:r.times\n        out.ok || return out\n        SimplePipelines.forget_failures!(rt)\n        out = SimplePipelines.exec(r.node, rt, scope, input)   # bypass the memo: run again\n    end\n    out\nend\n\nSimplePipelines.children(r::Repeat) = SimplePipelines.AbstractNode[r.node]\nSimplePipelines.dag(pr::SimplePipelines.DagPrinter, r::Repeat, prefix, cont) =\n    SimplePipelines.composite(pr, r, prefix, cont, \"⟳ \", :yellow, \"Repeat ×$(r.times)\")\n\nchildren makes steps, count_steps and print_dag work. These are internals, not part of the stable API.","title":"Adding a node type"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Code-conventions","page":"Development","text":"The repository's standards are in .cursor/rules/julia-standards.mdc. In short: dispatch instead of isa, no leading underscores, no try outside run_safely, validation in constructors, and small named functions. See Design for the architecture and the reasons behind the type layout.","title":"Code conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Control-flow","page":"Control flow","text":"Control-flow nodes wrap other nodes with a policy. They compose with everything else.","title":"Control flow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Retry","page":"Control flow","text":"download = @step download([] => \"data.tar\") = \"curl -fsSL -o {output} https://example.org/data.tar\"\n\ndownload^3                         # up to three attempts\nRetry(download, 5; delay=10)       # five attempts, 10 s apart\n\nOnly the parts that failed run again: a successful upstream step inside the retried node is not repeated.","title":"Retry"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Fallback","page":"Control flow","text":"fast = @step fast(\"in.txt\" => \"out.txt\") = \"fast_tool {input} > {output}\"\nslow = @step slow(\"in.txt\" => \"out.txt\") = \"slow_tool {input} > {output}\"\n\nfast | slow\nRetry(fast, 2) | slow\n\nThe fallback runs only if the primary fails. A pipeline that recovers through a fallback succeeds (success(result) is true), while the primary's failure stays visible in the results.","title":"Fallback"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Branch","page":"Control flow","text":"Branch(() -> filesize(\"data.txt\") > 10^9, big_file_path, small_file_path)\n@branch isfile(\"cache.bin\") load_cache rebuild          # condition written inline\n\nThe condition runs when the branch is reached. It may take the upstream value:\n\ndetect = @step detect = () -> occursin(\"PE\", read(\"meta.txt\", String))\ndetect >> Branch(paired -> paired, paired_end_steps, single_end_steps)\n\nIt must return a Bool; anything else, or an exception, is reported as a failure.","title":"Branch"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Timeout","page":"Control flow","text":"Timeout(long_step, 3600)\n\nIf the node is not done in time, the timeout fails with kind :timed_out. Commands still running are terminated, together with everything they started (a shell, a pipe of tools, their children). Steps inside the node that have not started yet are not started. A Julia function that is already running cannot be stopped safely; the pipeline stops waiting for it and gives its CPU slots and memory charge to other steps (the function keeps running in the background until it returns).\n\nA step's own timeout (@step s(; timeout=600) = ...) does the same for its command, without a Timeout node.","title":"Timeout"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/control-flow/#Force","page":"Control flow","text":"Force(step)                  # run even if up to date\nrun(pipeline; force=true)    # the same, for everything\n\nNext: Running and freshness.","title":"Force"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Design","page":"Design","text":"This page is for contributors. It explains how the package is put together and why.","title":"Design"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Concepts","page":"Design","text":"There are three kinds of objects:\n\nNodes (AbstractNode) are what runs: Step (one command or function), composites built by operators (Sequence, Parallel, Fallback, Retry, Branch, Timeout, Force, Resourced), and two lazy nodes that are expanded when they run: ForEach (fan-out) and Workflow (rules resolved against the filesystem).\nRules (Rule) are templates. They are not nodes: they become Steps inside a Workflow. Using a rule where a node is expected wraps it in a Workflow.\nResults: StepResult per step and RunResult per run.\n\nEverything a user writes reduces to these, so there is one execution path, one freshness model and one way to report errors.","title":"Concepts"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Source-layout","page":"Design","text":"file contents\nshell.jl Shell, sh, the bash strict-mode invocation, shell quoting\npatterns.jl wildcard Patterns, globbing, discover, expand, template rendering\nnodes.jl Step and composite nodes; constructors validate their input\nrules.jl Rule, gather, Workflow\noperators.jl >>, &, |, ^\nmacros.jl @step, @rule, @branch, @workflow\nresults.jl StepFailure, StepResult, RunResult, Outcome, run_safely\nresolve.jl rule resolution and pattern tracing; plan\nstate.jl freshness and the recipe file\nguard.jl, procfs.jl, policy.jl the resource guard process: /proc and cgroup readers, decide, guard_main\nscheduler.jl Need, Grant, admission control (Pool), Watchdog (Timeout deadlines)\nlink.jl the driver's side of the guard: starting it, the protocol, interrupts, guard loss\nruntime.jl Runtime, run lifecycle, memoization, recording\ncommands.jl running one step's work: rendering at launch, spawning, outcomes\nexec_step.jl exec(::Step): freshness, admission, eviction and requeue, results\nexec_nodes.jl exec for composite and lazy nodes\nlogging.jl, display.jl progress output, show, print_dag\nrun.jl run and check","title":"Source layout"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Execution","page":"Design","text":"run builds a Runtime (budgets, memo, results, state) and calls run_node(root, runtime, scope, input). run_node memoizes; exec dispatches on the node type. A Scope carries what is inherited down the tree: the force flag, default resources, the active Watchdog, the scheduling priority and the log tag.\n\nEach node returns an Outcome(ok, value); the value is what >> passes on. Parallel, ForEach and Job spawn one task per branch. Tasks are cheap, and concurrency is limited at the leaves instead: a step must acquire! its threads and memory from the Pool before it runs. Limiting at the leaves keeps the limit global however the graph is nested, and it cannot deadlock, because waiting branches hold nothing.\n\nThe pool admits waiters in priority order. A step's priority is its position in the tree (the vector of branch indices on the path to it), so earlier samples go first. The head of the queue blocks smaller requests behind it, which prevents a large step from starving.","title":"Execution"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Memoization","page":"Design","text":"run_node memoizes on (node, input): the first visitor executes, concurrent visitors wait on a channel, and later visitors get the stored outcome. A step with declared outputs is keyed by its outputs instead, so two distinct Step objects that write the same file run once. Memoizing composites, not just steps, keeps a shared sub-DAG linear in its size. Retry clears failed entries before each new attempt.","title":"Memoization"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Steps","page":"Design","text":"exec(::Step):\n\nstaleness decides whether the step must run, under the runtime lock.\nneed computes the step's Need: threads (at most jobs), the memory limit (declared or inherited mem_mb, lowered to the run's budget), the admission charge (the limit, or 256 MiB) and the timeout.\nIn check mode, the step's template is rendered to catch errors, and the step is recorded as planned and its outputs added to the pending set.\nOtherwise the step acquires a Grant; perform renders the template from the grant, creates the output directories, checks the inputs, runs the work and checks the outputs, and the step releases the grant.\nA command the guard stopped to free memory comes back as Evicted: the step is queued again with a charge based on what it used, and its third attempt runs alone. Otherwise finish! records the result, updates the recipe state, and removes partial outputs when the step failed.\n\nCommands run with run(...; wait=false) and stream stdout and stderr through pipes. Readers keep at most 32 MiB of stdout and the last 16 KiB of stderr. Every leaf of a command starts in its own session (setsid) with the thread variables set. Its leader pids are read while holding the iolock, so a command that exits at once cannot be reaped before it is registered, and Ctrl-C is deferred meanwhile.","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#The-resource-guard","page":"Design","text":"Enforcement cannot live in the Julia process that runs the pipeline: a busy function step, the garbage collector or a single thread would delay it, and processes would outlive Julia when it is killed. The guard is a separate Julia process (one thread), one per Julia session, started by the first run. It runs this package's precompiled code (using SimplePipelines; guard_main(), about 0.4 s); while the package is being precompiled or its cache is stale it compiles guard.jl itself at -O0 instead. It shares the session's process group, so Ctrl-C reaches it; it ignores SIGHUP and (on Linux) SIGTERM, and kills everything when its stdin closes, which is how it notices that Julia died. All its tasks defer Ctrl-C, which it handles between ticks.\n\nThe driver writes one line per event (run, step, sess, done, term, endrun) and a reader task handles the guard's lines (ready, sample, gone, warn, bye). Each tick (0.5 s, 0.1 s near a limit) the guard scans /proc, attributes processes to steps by session (and setsid'd children by parent), sums RssAnon + RssShmem + VmSwap per step, and reads available memory through every cgroup level. The pure function decide turns that view into terminations: per-step limits and deadlines, eviction under pressure (two samples, never a lone step, victims that exceed their charge first), immediate kills at the critical level, and leftover processes once a step's leaders have exited. A step is reported gone only when none of its processes is left.\n\nSamples feed the Pool: admission requires free CPU slots, the budget, and charge ≤ available − 2·reserve − unused charges of running steps. A stale sample or a paused guard stops admission; waiting longer than 120 s with nothing running refuses the waiters instead of hanging. The guard fails closed: if it cannot start or exits, the driver kills the commands itself and stops the run.","title":"The resource guard"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Errors","page":"Design","text":"User code runs inside run_safely, the package's single try/catch. It turns exceptions into failed outcomes carrying a StepFailure, and on Ctrl-C it calls interrupt_all! (refuse waiters, wake memo waiters, save state, kill every command) before rethrowing. The guard has no exception boundary at all: an error ends it, and the run stops. Locked sections defer Ctrl-C (with_lock) instead of using finally. Every other function reports problems through return values: Unresolved in resolution, Outcome(false, …) during execution.","title":"Errors"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Resolution","page":"Design","text":"plan_workflow turns a Workflow into a DAG of Jobs (a step plus the jobs that produce its inputs).\n\nConcrete paths resolve backward. Candidate rules are sorted by specificity (characters captured by wildcards), and the first whose inputs resolve wins. A path no rule can build is a Leaf if it exists, and Unresolved with a reason otherwise. Results are cached per path, so shared inputs give shared jobs.\nPatterns (pattern targets, gather, output-less rules) are traced. A pattern is unified with every rule output that can produce it: its wildcards are replaced by probe tokens made of control characters, which cannot occur in rule literals. The rule's inputs are then rewritten in terms of the pattern's wildcards, traced recursively, and the resulting assignments natural-joined. Patterns no rule produces are globbed on disk. Globbing walks only directories the pattern can match.","title":"Resolution"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Latency","page":"Design","text":"The package loads in about 0.2 s. A first run adds about 0.5 s, most of it starting the guard; later runs add about 20 ms.\n\nNode, result and outcome types are not parametric, and the runtime files are compiled with @nospecialize. User functions and values come in endless types; specializing the runtime on them would recompile it for every new closure. The traversal uses dynamic dispatch either way, so this costs nothing at run time.\nLocks are taken through with_lock(f, lock, args...) with named functions rather than capturing closures, whose types would differ for every value.\nA PrecompileTools workload runs the common paths (steps, rules, gather, check, ForEach, control flow, display) during precompilation.\n\nPer-step overhead is about 30 µs for a Julia function. A shell step costs what starting bash costs (a few ms), plus about 1 ms for the guard's round trip.","title":"Latency"},{"category":"section","location":"design/#Conventions","page":"Design","text":"The code follows the repository's standards (.cursor/rules/julia-standards.mdc): multiple dispatch instead of isa branching, no leading underscores, no try beyond run_safely, and small named functions. Type parameters are used where something dispatches on them (ForEach{S} on its source, Candidate{E}). Fields that hold arbitrary user values (StepResult.result, Outcome.value) are deliberately untyped, for the latency reason above.","title":"Conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/quickref/#Quick-reference","page":"Quick reference","text":"","title":"Quick reference"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/quickref/#Steps","page":"Quick reference","text":"syntax meaning\n@step name = sh\"cmd\" / = \"cmd\" shell command (bash -e -o pipefail)\n@step name = sh(\"cmd $(x)\") with Julia interpolation\n@step name = () -> expr Julia function\n@step name = f(args) call deferred until the step runs\n@step name(in => out) = \"tool {input} > {output}\" declared paths and a template\n@step name([a, b] => [c]; threads=4, mem_mb=8000, timeout=600) = ... several paths, limits (MiB, seconds)\n@step name(in => out) = julia(\"script.jl {input} {output}\") Julia script in a separate, limited process\n@step name(in => out) = (i, o) -> ... function receiving the paths\n@step name(in) = ... inputs only\nStep(name, work, inputs, outputs; threads, mem_mb, timeout) constructor form\n\nPlaceholders: {input}, {output}, {input[i]}, {output[i]}, {threads}, {mem_mb}, {wildcard}; {{word}} is a literal {word}. {threads} and {mem_mb} are filled in when the step starts, with what it was granted.","title":"Steps"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/quickref/#Composition","page":"Quick reference","text":"syntax meaning value\na >> b then value of b (receives value of a)\na & b in parallel; write (a & b) >> c [va, vb]\na | b fallback value of the one that succeeded\na^n, Retry(a, n; delay) retry \nBranch(cond, a, b), @branch cond a b conditional \nTimeout(a, seconds) deadline; kills commands \nForce(a) ignore freshness \nwith_resources(a; threads, mem_mb, timeout) default limits \nForEach(items) do x ... end one branch per item vector\nForEach(\"dir/{w}.ext\") do w ... end one branch per file vector\nSequence(nodes...), Parallel(nodes...) function forms ","title":"Composition"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/quickref/#Rules-and-workflows","page":"Quick reference","text":"syntax meaning\n@rule name(\"in/{s}.x\" => \"out/{s}.y\"; threads) = \"cmd {input} > {output}\" rule\n@step name(\"in/{s}.x\" => \"out/{s}.y\") = ... the same (wildcards make a rule)\ngather(\"bam/{s}.bam\") input collecting every value of {s}\nr1 >> r2 >> r3 merge rules into a Workflow\nWorkflow(rules_and_steps...; targets, filter, name) explicit workflow\n@workflow \"name\" begin ... end workflow from a block\nrun(wf; targets=\"out/{s}.y\") pattern target: every s with sources\nrun(wf; filter = w -> w.s != \"ctl\") drop wildcard values\nexpand(\"out/{s}.y\"; s=[\"A\", \"B\"]) concrete paths\ndiscover(\"raw/{s}_R1.fq.gz\") wildcard values on disk\nplan(wf; targets) concrete DAG, without running","title":"Rules and workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"reference/quickref/#Running-and-inspecting","page":"Quick reference","text":"call meaning\nrun(x; jobs, mem_mb, min_free_mb, nice, guard, force, verbose, state_path) run; returns a RunResult\ncheck(x; ...) what would run and why; runs nothing\nprint_dag(x) tree with paths and commands\nsuccess(r), r.value, r[i] overall success, pipeline value, step results\nr[i].label, .status, .result, .duration, .peak_mb per-step fields\nsteps(x), count_steps(x) steps of a node\nis_fresh(step), clear_state!() freshness state","title":"Running and inspecting"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bioinformatics/#Examples:-bioinformatics","page":"Bioinformatics","text":"Sketches of complete workflows. They need the tools installed; the commands are the usual ones. A runnable version with placeholder commands is in examples/bioinformatics.jl.","title":"Examples: bioinformatics"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bioinformatics/#Variant-calling-for-every-sample","page":"Bioinformatics","text":"raw/{s}_R1.fq.gz ─┐\n                  ├─ trim ── align ── markdup ─┬─ index\nraw/{s}_R2.fq.gz ─┘                            └─ (all samples) ── call ── filter\n\nusing SimplePipelines\n\nconst REF = \"ref/GRCh38.fa\"\n\nindex_ref = @step index_ref(REF => REF * \".bwt\") = \"bwa index {input}\"\n\ntrim = @rule trim([\"raw/{s}_R1.fq.gz\", \"raw/{s}_R2.fq.gz\"] =>\n                  [\"trim/{s}_R1.fq.gz\", \"trim/{s}_R2.fq.gz\"]; threads=4) =\n    \"fastp -w {threads} -i {input[1]} -I {input[2]} -o {output[1]} -O {output[2]}\"\n\nalign = @rule align([\"trim/{s}_R1.fq.gz\", \"trim/{s}_R2.fq.gz\", REF * \".bwt\"] => \"bam/{s}.bam\";\n                    threads=8, mem_mb=16_000) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} -R '@RG\\\\tID:{s}\\\\tSM:{s}' $REF {input[1]} {input[2]} | \" *\n    \"samtools sort -@ 4 -m 1500M -o {output}\"\n\nmarkdup = @rule markdup(\"bam/{s}.bam\" => \"dedup/{s}.bam\"; threads=4) =\n    \"samtools markdup -@ {threads} {input} {output}\"\n\nindex = @rule index(\"dedup/{s}.bam\" => \"dedup/{s}.bam.bai\") = \"samtools index {input}\"\n\ncall = @rule call(gather(\"dedup/{s}.bam\") => \"calls/raw.vcf.gz\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bcftools mpileup --threads {threads} -f $REF {input} | \" *\n    \"bcftools call -mv -Oz -o {output}\"\n\nfilter_calls = @rule filter_calls(\"calls/raw.vcf.gz\" => \"calls/filtered.vcf.gz\") =\n    \"bcftools filter -i 'QUAL>=20' -Oz -o {output} {input}\"\n\nwf = Workflow(index_ref, trim, align, markdup, index, call, filter_calls; name=\"variants\")\n\ncheck(wf)                     # what will run for the samples on disk\nrun(wf; jobs=32, mem_mb=96_000)\n\nWhat this saves you:\n\nno sample list: samples are the pairs present in raw/, and a sample with only R1 is left out;\nthe reference is indexed once, before any alignment;\ncall waits for every sample and reruns when a sample is added;\nat most 32 threads and 96 000 MiB of memory are in use at any time, samples are finished a few at a time, and an alignment that grows beyond its 16 000 MiB is stopped instead of freezing the machine (bwa plus samtools sort's 4 × 1500 MiB stay well inside it);\na failed alignment removes its partial BAM, and bwa | samtools fails if either tool fails.\n\nOnly part of the samples: run(wf; targets=\"dedup/{s}.bam.bai\", filter = w -> startswith(w.s, \"P1_\")).","title":"Variant calling for every sample"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bioinformatics/#RNA-seq-quantification","page":"Bioinformatics","text":"quant = @rule quant([\"raw/{s}_R1.fq.gz\", \"raw/{s}_R2.fq.gz\"] => \"quant/{s}/abundance.tsv\"; threads=4) =\n    \"kallisto quant -t {threads} -i ref/transcripts.idx -o quant/{s} {input}\"\n\nmatrix = @rule matrix(gather(\"quant/{s}/abundance.tsv\") => \"counts.tsv\") =\n    function (files, output)\n        samples = [basename(dirname(f)) for f in files]\n        tables = [split.(readlines(f)[2:end], '\\t') for f in files]\n        open(output, \"w\") do io\n            println(io, join([\"target\"; samples], '\\t'))\n            for (i, row) in enumerate(first(tables))\n                println(io, join([row[1]; [t[i][4] for t in tables]], '\\t'))\n            end\n        end\n    end\n\nrun(quant >> matrix)\n\nThe Julia function receives the gathered abundance files as a vector and the output path; it runs once all quantifications are done.","title":"RNA-seq quantification"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/bioinformatics/#Immune-repertoire,-one-branch-per-donor","page":"Bioinformatics","text":"ForEach suits a per-donor chain written as ordinary Julia:\n\ndonors = ForEach(\"fastq/{donor}_R1.fq.gz\") do d\n    merge = @step merge([\"fastq/$(d)_R1.fq.gz\", \"fastq/$(d)_R2.fq.gz\"] => \"merged/$d.assembled.fastq\"; threads=4) =\n        \"pear -j {threads} -f {input[1]} -r {input[2]} -o merged/$d\"\n    fasta = @step fasta(\"merged/$d.assembled.fastq\" => \"fasta/$d.fasta\") = \"seqtk seq -A {input} > {output}\"\n    igblast = @step igblast(\"fasta/$d.fasta\" => \"igblast/$d.tsv\"; threads=4) =\n        \"igblastn -num_threads {threads} -query {input} -germline_db_V db/V -germline_db_D db/D \" *\n        \"-germline_db_J db/J -outfmt 19 -out {output}\"\n    merge >> fasta >> igblast\nend\n\nrun(donors)\n\nThe same workflow as rules (@rule merge(\"fastq/{d}_R1.fq.gz\" => ...)) would also let you build a single donor by its output name.","title":"Immune repertoire, one branch per donor"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#Examples:-basics","page":"Basics","text":"Short, runnable recipes. Each one works in an empty directory.","title":"Examples: basics"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#A-diamond","page":"Basics","text":"using SimplePipelines\n\nfetch   = @step fetch([] => \"data.csv\") = \"printf 'id,value\\n1,10\\n2,20\\n' > {output}\"\nrows    = @step rows(\"data.csv\" => \"rows.txt\") = \"tail -n +2 {input} | wc -l > {output}\"\ntotal   = @step total(\"data.csv\" => \"total.txt\") = \"tail -n +2 {input} | cut -d, -f2 | paste -sd+ | bc > {output}\"\nsummary = @step summary([\"rows.txt\", \"total.txt\"] => \"summary.txt\") = \"paste {input} > {output}\"\n\nrun(fetch >> (rows & total) >> summary)","title":"A diamond"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#Julia-and-shell-together","page":"Basics","text":"numbers = @step numbers([] => \"numbers.txt\") = \"seq 1 100 > {output}\"\nstats = @step stats(\"numbers.txt\" => \"stats.txt\") = function (input, output)\n    xs = parse.(Int, readlines(input))\n    write(output, \"mean=$(sum(xs) / length(xs)) max=$(maximum(xs))\\n\")\nend\nshow_stats = @step show_stats = paths -> read(only(paths), String)\n\nrun(numbers >> stats >> show_stats).value       # \"mean=50.5 max=100\\n\"","title":"Julia and shell together"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#Values-without-files","page":"Basics","text":"a = @step a = () -> 2\nb = @step b = () -> 3\nproduct = @step product = xs -> prod(xs)\n\nrun((a & b) >> product).value                   # 6","title":"Values without files"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#One-step-per-file","page":"Basics","text":"for name in [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\n    write(\"$name.txt\", \"hello $name\\n\")\nend\n\nupper = @rule upper(\"{name}.txt\" => \"upper/{name}.txt\") = \"tr a-z A-Z < {input} > {output}\"\nrun(upper)                       # three steps: upper[name=a], upper[name=b], upper[name=c]","title":"One step per file"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#Retry,-fallback-and-timeout","page":"Basics","text":"flaky   = @step flaky = sh\"test $((RANDOM % 3)) -eq 0\"\nbackup  = @step backup = sh\"echo using the backup\"\npipeline = Timeout(flaky^5 | backup, 60)\n\nrun(pipeline)","title":"Retry, fallback and timeout"},{"category":"section","location":"examples/basics/#Inspect-first","page":"Basics","text":"print_dag(fetch >> (rows & total) >> summary)\ncheck(fetch >> (rows & total) >> summary)","title":"Inspect first"},{"category":"section","location":"#SimplePipelines.jl","page":"Home","text":"Incremental, parallel pipelines for shell commands and Julia functions, with Snakemake-style wildcard rules.\n\nSimplePipelines takes the plumbing out of preprocessing pipelines. Declare what each step reads and writes; the package works out the order, runs independent work in parallel within your CPU and memory budget, skips what is already up to date, and tells you exactly what failed and why.\n\nusing SimplePipelines\n\ntrim  = @rule trim(\"raw/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{sample}.fq.gz\"; threads=4) =\n    \"fastp -w {threads} -i {input} -o {output}\"\nalign = @rule align([\"trim/{sample}.fq.gz\", \"ref.fa\"] => \"bam/{sample}.bam\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input[1]} | samtools sort -o {output}\"\ncalls = @rule calls(gather(\"bam/{sample}.bam\") => \"calls.vcf\") =\n    \"bcftools mpileup -f ref.fa {input} | bcftools call -mv -o {output}\"\n\ncheck(trim >> align >> calls)     # what would run, and why\nrun(trim >> align >> calls)       # one trim and align per sample on disk, then one variant call\n\nThere is no sample list to maintain: the samples are whatever raw/*_R1.fq.gz files exist. Add a sample and rerun, and only the new sample plus the variant call run again.","title":"SimplePipelines.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-it-does-for-you","page":"Home","text":"Wildcards end to end. {sample} in a path is a wildcard. Targets can be patterns (\"bam/{sample}.bam\"): values are discovered by tracing the rules back to the source files. gather(...) collects every sample into one step.\nIncremental builds that are correct. A step is skipped only when its outputs exist, are newer than its inputs, and were made by the same command. Edit a command and the affected steps rerun. Outputs of a failed step are removed, so a half-written file never looks finished.\nIt cannot freeze your computer. A resource guard measures every command and terminates one that exceeds its mem_mb or timeout. When memory runs low, new steps wait, and the pipeline's own commands are stopped and queued again before the system starts swapping. Steps leave no processes behind, and Ctrl-C, a closed terminal or a crash of Julia kill everything the pipeline started. Container and cluster (cgroup) limits are respected.\nErrors you can act on. Shell commands run with bash -e -o pipefail, so a failure inside a pipe fails the step. Typos in wildcards and unknown placeholders are caught when the rule is defined. When a target cannot be built, you get the chain of missing files.\nLow latency. Loading takes about 0.2 s. The runtime is compiled once, so your own functions add almost no compile time on the first run.","title":"What it does for you"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Getting started: a first pipeline in five minutes.\nSteps and Composing pipelines: the building blocks.\nWildcard rules and workflows: the part that saves the most code in bioinformatics.\nRunning and freshness: what run and check do.\nResource limits and safety: how the pipeline keeps your computer responsive, and what to declare.\n\nThe quick reference fits the whole API on one page. If you are upgrading from 0.3, read the migration notes.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Wildcard-rules-and-workflows","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"Most preprocessing applies the same steps to many files. A rule is a step template whose paths contain {wildcards}; a workflow is a set of rules that SimplePipelines turns into concrete steps by matching file names.","title":"Wildcard rules and workflows"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Rules","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"using SimplePipelines\n\ntrim = @rule trim(\"raw/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{sample}.fq.gz\"; threads=4) =\n    \"fastp -w {threads} -i {input} -o {output}\"\n\nRead this as: \"to make trim/X.fq.gz, run fastp on raw/X_R1.fq.gz\". A wildcard matches one path component, so it never contains /. @step with a wildcard path builds the same thing, so you can use whichever reads better.\n\nRules are checked when they are defined:\n\nevery input wildcard must appear in the outputs, so {smaple} vs {sample} is an error, not a silent mismatch;\nall outputs must use the same wildcards;\nunknown placeholders in the command are errors.\n\nThe command can use {input}, {output}, {input[i]}, {output[i]}, {threads}, {mem_mb} and the wildcards themselves ({sample}). A function rule receives one argument per declared input (a Vector{String} for a gather), then the outputs:\n\nfilter_reads = @rule filter_reads(\"tsv/{sample}.tsv\" => \"filtered/{sample}.tsv\") =\n    function (input, output)\n        rows = filter(l -> !occursin(\"\\tNA\\t\", l), readlines(input))\n        write(output, join(rows, \"\\n\"))\n    end","title":"Rules"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Running-rules","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"Running one rule builds its outputs for every input that exists:\n\nrun(trim)          # one step per raw/*_R1.fq.gz\n\nChain rules with >> (or &; for rules they mean the same thing). The result is a Workflow: a single node that is resolved into concrete steps when it runs.\n\nalign = @rule align([\"trim/{sample}.fq.gz\", \"ref.fa\"] => \"bam/{sample}.bam\"; threads=8) =\n    \"bwa mem -t {threads} ref.fa {input[1]} | samtools sort -o {output}\"\nindex = @rule index(\"bam/{sample}.bam\" => \"bam/{sample}.bam.bai\") = \"samtools index {input}\"\n\nwf = trim >> align >> index\nrun(wf)\n\nNothing lists the samples. The workflow builds the outputs of every rule that no other rule consumes (here bam/{sample}.bam.bai), and finds the samples by tracing back through align and trim to the files that exist under raw/. Order does not matter: dependencies come from the file names, so index >> align >> trim is the same workflow.\n\nEach sample becomes its own chain of steps, and samples run in parallel within your jobs budget. The scheduler prefers to finish a sample before starting the next, so results arrive early and intermediate files do not pile up.","title":"Running rules"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Targets","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"Pass targets to choose what to build. A target is a path or a pattern:\n\nrun(wf; targets=\"bam/A.bam.bai\")                       # one sample\nrun(wf; targets=\"bam/{sample}.bam\")                    # every sample, stop after align\nrun(wf; targets=expand(\"bam/{s}.bam.bai\"; s=[\"A\", \"B\"]))\nrun(trim, align; targets=\"bam/A.bam\")                  # rules given directly\n\nA pattern target gets its wildcard values from the source files, like the automatic targets. A sample whose sources are incomplete (an R1 without its R2, say) is not a target. To drop values, pass a filter:\n\nrun(wf; filter = w -> w.sample != \"control\")\nrun(wf; filter = w -> parse(Int, w.rep) < 100)\n\nThe filter receives a Wildcards value: read fields as w.sample or w[\"sample\"].","title":"Targets"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Collecting-all-samples:-gather","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"A merge step needs every sample. Wrap its input in gather: wildcards that do not appear in the outputs are collected over every value.\n\ncalls = @rule calls(gather(\"bam/{sample}.bam\") => \"calls.vcf\") =\n    \"bcftools mpileup -f ref.fa {input} | bcftools call -mv -o {output}\"\n\nrun(trim >> align >> calls)\n\nThe BAM files do not have to exist yet. The workflow traces them back to the raw files, so calls depends on the align step of every sample. When a new sample appears, calls reruns, because its list of inputs changed. Wildcards that do appear in the outputs still split the work, which gives one merge per group:\n\n@rule per_donor(gather(\"bam/{donor}_{rep}.bam\") => \"merged/{donor}.bam\") =\n    \"samtools merge {output} {input}\"","title":"Collecting all samples: gather"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Concrete-steps-and-output-less-rules","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"A workflow can contain concrete steps with outputs, for example a reference index that every sample needs. It is built once, before the samples that need it:\n\nindex_ref = @step index_ref(\"ref.fa\" => \"ref.fa.bwt\") = \"bwa index {input}\"\nalign = @rule align([\"trim/{s}.fq.gz\", \"ref.fa.bwt\"] => \"bam/{s}.bam\") =\n    \"bwa mem ref.fa {input[1]} > {output}\"\nrun(Workflow(index_ref, trim, align))\n\nA rule without outputs, such as a validation or upload step, runs once for every match of its inputs:\n\nvalidate = @rule validate(\"bam/{s}.bam\") = \"samtools quickcheck {input}\"\nrun(trim >> align >> validate)","title":"Concrete steps and output-less rules"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#How-a-target-is-resolved","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"When several rules can produce a path, the most specific one wins (the one whose wildcards match the fewest characters), as long as its inputs can be satisfied. For A.sorted.bam, a rule for {x}.sorted.bam beats one for {x}.bam, regardless of the order you wrote them in.\n\nAs in Make, an existing file that no applicable rule can rebuild is used as it is. When a target cannot be built, the error names the whole chain:\n\ncannot build `bam/Z.bam`:\n  rule `align[sample=Z]` needs `trim/Z.fq.gz`\n  rule `trim[sample=Z]` needs `raw/Z_R1.fq.gz`\n  `raw/Z_R1.fq.gz` does not exist and no rule produces it","title":"How a target is resolved"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Inspecting-a-workflow","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"discover(\"raw/{sample}_R1.fq.gz\")   # what does the pattern match on disk?\nprint_dag(wf)                        # the concrete steps, with paths and commands\ncheck(wf)                            # what would run, and why\nplan(wf; targets=\"bam/A.bam\")       # the concrete DAG as a node\n\nSteps in logs and results are labelled with their wildcard values: align[sample=A].","title":"Inspecting a workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/rules/#Workflows-inside-pipelines","page":"Wildcard rules and workflows","text":"A workflow is a node, so it composes with steps. It is resolved when it runs, so it sees files that earlier steps created:\n\nfetch = @step fetch([] => \"raw/manifest.txt\") = \"download_run.sh raw/\"\nreport = @step report = bams -> write(\"report.txt\", join(bams, \"\\n\"))\n\nrun(fetch >> (trim >> align) >> report)    # report receives the BAM paths\n\nFor a workflow in one block, use @workflow:\n\nwf = @workflow \"rnaseq\" begin\n    @rule trim(\"raw/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"trim/{s}.fq.gz\") = \"fastp -i {input} -o {output}\"\n    @rule quant(\"trim/{s}.fq.gz\" => \"quant/{s}/abundance.tsv\"; threads=4) =\n        \"kallisto quant -t {threads} -i idx -o quant/{s} --single -l 200 -s 20 {input}\"\nend\n\nNext: Fan-out with ForEach.","title":"Workflows inside pipelines"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Doctests","page":"Doctests","text":"These examples are run by Documenter on every documentation build, so they are guaranteed to match the package.","title":"Doctests"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Values-flow-with","page":"Doctests","text":"julia> half = @step half = () -> 21;\n\njulia> double = @step double = x -> 2x;\n\njulia> run(half >> double; verbose=false).value\n42\n\njulia> run((sh\"echo a\" & sh\"echo b\") >> (@step n = v -> length(v)); verbose=false).value\n2","title":"Values flow with >>"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Files,-templates-and-freshness","page":"Doctests","text":"julia> make = @step make([] => \"data.txt\") = \"seq 1 5 > {output}\";\n\njulia> total = @step total(\"data.txt\" => \"total.txt\") = \"paste -sd+ {input} | bc > {output}\";\n\njulia> r = run(make >> total; verbose=false);\n\njulia> success(r), [s.status for s in r]\n(true, [:done, :done])\n\njulia> read(\"total.txt\", String)\n\"15\\n\"\n\njulia> [s.status for s in run(make >> total; verbose=false)]\n2-element Vector{Symbol}:\n :skipped\n :skipped","title":"Files, templates and freshness"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Failures-are-results","page":"Doctests","text":"julia> r = run(sh\"false | cat\"; verbose=false);\n\njulia> success(r), r[1].result.kind\n(false, :process_failed)\n\njulia> run(sh\"false\" | sh\"echo recovered\"; verbose=false).value\n\"recovered\\n\"","title":"Failures are results"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Rules-and-gather","page":"Doctests","text":"julia> mkpath(\"raw\"); foreach(s -> write(\"raw/$s.txt\", s), [\"a\", \"b\"]);\n\njulia> upper = @rule upper(\"raw/{s}.txt\" => \"up/{s}.txt\") = \"tr a-z A-Z < {input} > {output}\";\n\njulia> joined = @rule joined(gather(\"up/{s}.txt\") => \"all.txt\") = \"cat {input} > {output}\";\n\njulia> r = run(upper >> joined; verbose=false);\n\njulia> [s.label for s in r if s.status === :done] |> sort\n3-element Vector{String}:\n \"joined\"\n \"upper[s=a]\"\n \"upper[s=b]\"\n\njulia> read(\"all.txt\", String)\n\"AB\"","title":"Rules and gather"},{"category":"section","location":"doctests/#Wildcards-on-disk","page":"Doctests","text":"julia> mkpath(\"fq\"); foreach(s -> write(\"fq/$(s)_R1.fq\", \"\"), [\"S1\", \"S2\"]);\n\njulia> [w.sample for w in discover(\"fq/{sample}_R1.fq\")]\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"S1\"\n \"S2\"\n\njulia> expand(\"bam/{sample}.bam\"; sample=[\"S1\", \"S2\"])\n2-element Vector{String}:\n \"bam/S1.bam\"\n \"bam/S2.bam\"","title":"Wildcards on disk"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Running-and-freshness","page":"Running and freshness","text":"","title":"Running and freshness"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#run","page":"Running and freshness","text":"results = run(pipeline; jobs=16, mem_mb=64_000, force=false, verbose=true)\n\nkeyword default meaning\njobs CPUs this process may use CPU slots; a step uses as many as its threads\nmem_mb 80% of the memory this process may use memory budget in MiB for the steps running at once\nmin_free_mb, nice, guard  see Resource limits and safety\nforce false run every step, even if up to date\nverbose true print progress\nstate_path \".pipeline_state\" where recipes of built files are remembered\n\nFor rules and workflows, run also takes targets and filter (see rules).\n\nWhen a step fails, its branch stops; independent branches keep going, so one bad sample does not cost you the others. Rerun after fixing it: everything that succeeded is skipped.","title":"run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Results","page":"Running and freshness","text":"run returns a RunResult, a vector of StepResults in completion order, with:\n\nsuccess, also success(results): whether the pipeline as a whole succeeded;\nvalue: the value of the pipeline (for example, what a final function returned);\nduration in seconds.\n\nEach StepResult has label (such as align[sample=A]), status (:done, :skipped, :failed), success, duration, peak_mb (peak memory of its command), inputs, outputs and result: stdout for a shell step, the return value for a function, or a StepFailure when the step failed.\n\nfailed = filter(r -> !r.success, results)\nforeach(r -> println(r.label, \": \", r.result.message), failed)\n\nA StepFailure has a kind (:process_failed, :memory_limit, :timed_out, :exception, :missing_input, :missing_output, …), a message that says what to change, and a detail with the end of stderr or the top of the Julia stack trace.","title":"Results"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#check","page":"Running and freshness","text":"check(pipeline)\n\ncheck takes the same arguments as run but runs nothing. It expands ForEach nodes, resolves workflows, and reports each step as :planned with the reason it would run, :skipped if up to date, or :failed when something is wrong, such as an input that does not exist and that no earlier step produces.\n\n═══ Check: pipeline ═══\n▶ align[sample=C]  (missing output bam/C.bam)\n▶ calls  (input bam/C.bam will be rebuilt)\n═══ 2 to run, 4 up to date, 0 failed in 0.01s ═══\n\nprint_dag shows the structure, with ForEach nodes expanded and workflows resolved into their concrete steps.","title":"check"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Freshness","page":"Running and freshness","text":"A step with declared outputs is up to date, and skipped, when:\n\nevery output exists,\nno input is newer than the oldest output, and\nthe outputs were last made by the same recipe: the same command template, the same inputs and, for a rule, the same wildcard values.\n\nThe third rule is what makes editing a command rerun the affected steps, and what makes a merge rerun when a sample is added. Recipes are remembered in .pipeline_state, a small text file with one line per output. If a file has no record, because it was made before the first run or the state file was deleted, the first two rules decide, and the record is added. Julia functions are tracked by their inputs and outputs only; after changing a function, use force=true or Force.\n\nSteps without declared outputs always run. Their value, such as the result of a function, is therefore always fresh when passed downstream.\n\nWhen a step fails, the output files it wrote are deleted, so a partial file never passes as finished. clear_state! forgets the recorded recipes and is_fresh tells you whether a step would be skipped.","title":"Freshness"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Scheduling","page":"Running and freshness","text":"The scheduler admits a step only when its resources fit:\n\njobs is the number of CPU slots. A step takes threads slots (default 1), so at most jobs threads are busy, however deeply parallel nodes are nested. A step asking for more than jobs gets jobs.\nmem_mb caps the memory charged to running steps: their mem_mb, or 256 MiB for a step that declares none. A step also waits until that much memory is actually free. A step that does not fit runs alone rather than waiting forever.\n\n@rule sort_bam(\"bam/{s}.bam\" => \"sorted/{s}.bam\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000) =\n    \"samtools sort -@ {threads} -m 1500M -o {output} {input}\"\n\nmem_mb is also the step's memory limit, enforced while it runs; see Resource limits and safety.\n\nWhen several steps are waiting, the one earliest in the pipeline (for example, the next step of the first sample) goes first. Samples are finished a few at a time rather than all first steps being run first.\n\nShell commands run concurrently even when Julia has a single thread. Julia function steps run on Julia threads, so start Julia with -t auto if they do heavy work, or better, run heavy Julia code with julia so it can be limited.","title":"Scheduling"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/running/#Output","page":"Running and freshness","text":"A command's stdout is captured up to 32 MiB. A command that prints more is stopped and fails with :stdout_limit: redirect large output to a file and declare it as an output. Only the last 16 KiB of stderr is kept.\nValues passed between steps stay in memory until run returns, as part of the results. For large data, write files and pass paths.\n\nNext: Resource limits and safety.","title":"Output"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Resource-limits-and-safety","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"A pipeline must never freeze your computer. SimplePipelines therefore runs every command under a resource guard: a small separate process, started by the first run, that measures each command the pipeline starts and stops the ones that use too much memory or time. It works even while Julia itself is busy, and it kills everything the pipeline started if Julia is interrupted or dies. There is nothing to set up.","title":"Resource limits and safety"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Declaring-what-a-step-needs","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"@rule sort_bam(\"bam/{s}.bam\" => \"sorted/{s}.bam\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000, timeout=3600) =\n    \"samtools sort -@ {threads} -m 1500M -o {output} {input}\"\n\nthreads: CPU slots the step takes (default 1). {threads} in the template is this number, and its commands see OMP_NUM_THREADS, OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS, MKL_NUM_THREADS, RAYON_NUM_THREADS, JULIA_NUM_THREADS, GOMAXPROCS and similar variables set to it, so multithreaded libraries do not start one thread per core in every step. These variables are advice that a tool may ignore; pass {threads} to tools that take a thread flag. nproc follows OMP_NUM_THREADS, so $(nproc) in a script equals the step's threads.\nmem_mb: the step's memory limit in MiB. The step starts when that much fits in the free memory not promised to running steps (or alone, when nothing else runs and memory is not low), and it is terminated if its processes use more (:memory_limit). {mem_mb} in the template is the same number. Give the tool about 80% of it, because tools need memory beyond their own setting, and per-thread settings multiply: samtools sort -@ 4 -m 1500M may use 4 × 1500 MiB.\ntimeout: seconds. A command still running after that long is terminated (:timed_out). Julia functions cannot be stopped: wrap such a step in a Timeout to stop waiting for it (it is abandoned and its resources freed).\n\nwith_resources(node; threads, mem_mb, timeout) sets defaults for every step inside node that does not declare its own.","title":"Declaring what a step needs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#What-the-guard-does","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"situation what happens\na command uses more memory than its mem_mb terminated within a fraction of a second; the step fails with :memory_limit\na command runs longer than its timeout SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 5 s later; the step fails with :timed_out\na Timeout node expires its commands are terminated; its steps that have not started never start\nfree memory gets low new steps wait (⏸ waiting for memory); if it stays low for 120 s while nothing of the pipeline runs, the waiting steps fail with :out_of_memory instead of waiting forever\nfree memory keeps falling the pipeline's own commands are stopped, those using more than they declared first, and queued again when memory is free (↺); a step that does not fit even when it runs alone fails with :out_of_memory\nother programs use the memory nothing is stopped; the run waits and says so\na step leaves background processes running they are terminated and the step fails\nCtrl-C, the terminal is closed, Julia is killed every command the pipeline started is killed\n\nMemory is measured per step, summed over every process the step starts, as memory the system cannot reclaim: anonymous memory, shared memory and swap. Page cache from reading large files does not count. Free memory takes container and cgroup limits into account (Docker, Slurm, Kubernetes, systemd), so the pipeline stays inside the allocation of a cluster job.\n\nSteps that declare no mem_mb have no per-step limit, but they are measured and protected like the others: they are charged 256 MiB when they start, and they are the first to be stopped when memory runs out because they use more than they declared. Declare mem_mb for memory-hungry tools so they start only when there is room for them.\n\nStepResult.peak_mb holds each command's peak memory, and the log shows it: ✓ align[s=A] (12.3s, peak 3.2 GiB). Use it to choose mem_mb.","title":"What the guard does"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Run-settings","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"run(pipeline; jobs=16, mem_mb=64_000, min_free_mb=0, nice=10, guard=true)\n\nkeyword default meaning\njobs CPUs this process may use CPU slots; a step takes as many as its threads\nmem_mb 80% of the memory this process may use budget for the steps running at once, in MiB (0: none); a step's mem_mb above it is lowered to it\nmin_free_mb automatic: 5% of memory, between 256 and 2048 MiB memory to keep free: below twice this new steps wait, below it commands are stopped\nnice 10 priority of commands (0–19), so the computer stays responsive\nguard true (false on Windows) false runs without the guard: no memory limits, step timeouts or memory-pressure handling, and no cleanup if Julia is killed; Timeout nodes and the cleanup at the end of each step still work\n\nThe CPU and memory defaults follow the process's CPU affinity and cgroup limits, so jobs and mem_mb are right inside containers and cluster jobs.","title":"Run settings"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Julia-code","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"Julia function steps run inside your Julia session. Their mem_mb is reserved when they start but cannot be enforced, and they cannot be stopped. Put heavy Julia work in a script and run it with julia:\n\n@step counts(\"reads.tsv\" => \"counts.tsv\"; threads=4, mem_mb=8000) =\n    julia(\"--project=scripts scripts/count.jl {input} {output}\")\n\nThe script runs in a new Julia process: this Julia, the active project, --threads={threads} and a --heap-size-hint below mem_mb, followed by your arguments (your flags win). It is limited, measured and stopped like any command. The paths are in ARGS.\n\nCommands started with run(...) inside a Julia function belong to your Julia session and are not tracked; return the command from the function instead, and the step runs it.","title":"Julia code"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Rules-for-commands","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"A step ends when all its processes have exited. If the main command finishes and leaves processes behind, they are terminated and the step fails: end scripts that start background jobs with wait.\nCommands have no terminal: stdin is /dev/null, so a tool waiting for input fails instead of hanging.\nCommands run at nice 10 and are the system's preferred victims if it runs out of memory despite everything, so Julia and your desktop survive.\nAn output process substitution such as tee >(sort > x) is not waited for by bash, so a still running sort counts as a leftover. Write to a file and process it in a following command instead.\nA process that must outlive the step does not belong in a pipeline step. One that escapes anyway (below) must not keep the step's output open: the step then fails instead of hanging.","title":"Rules for commands"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Limits","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"Linux has every guarantee above. On macOS timeouts, cleanup and Ctrl-C work, but memory is not measured. On Windows the guard is not available: runs say so and work without it.\nMemory is sampled every 0.5 s (0.1 s near a limit), so a command that allocates very fast can overshoot briefly. If the system runs out of memory in that moment, its out-of-memory killer picks the command, which fails with :out_of_memory.\nA process escapes the guard when it leaves the step's session and its parent exits within one sampling interval, for example a command setsid -f cmd or a daemon that double-forks. setsid -w cmd is tracked.\nContainers started through a daemon (docker run, podman run with a service) run outside the step's processes: they get no memory limit and are not cleaned up. Use apptainer/singularity, which run as child processes, or give the container its own limit (docker run --rm --init --memory={mem_mb}m ...).\nFiles written to memory-backed filesystems such as /dev/shm count as used memory but belong to no step.\nSeveral runs at the same time in one Julia session share the guard but not their budgets.\nThe guard is one small Julia process per Julia session: it starts in about 0.4 s with the first run, uses about 120 MiB of its own memory (about 210 MB resident, most of it shared with Julia's libraries), and stops with Julia.","title":"Limits"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/resources/#Failures","page":"Resource limits and safety","text":"kind meaning\n:memory_limit the step used more than its mem_mb; raise it, or lower the tool's own memory setting\n:out_of_memory the computer ran out of memory for this step, or the system's out-of-memory killer stopped it\n:timed_out the step's timeout or an enclosing Timeout expired\n:process_failed the command failed, left background processes running, or was killed from outside\n:stopped the run was interrupted, or the guard exited unexpectedly\n:template a placeholder cannot be filled, such as {mem_mb} without a declared mem_mb\n\nIf the guard cannot start or exits unexpectedly, the run stops and kills its commands, because steps must not run without protection.\n\nNext: Examples.","title":"Failures"}]
}
